\title{Terminology for the Division Algorithm}
\date{2026-04-06T13:54:02Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{In this definition, #{q} is called the \em{quotient} and #{r} the \em{remainder}. We write ##{\begin{align*}
  q &= a \div d \\
  r &= a \mod d
\end{align*}} The #{\div} operator is written \code{/} in Java and C/C++, and \code{//} in Python and Disco; the #{\mod} operator is written \code{\startverb%\stopverb} in many programming languages, and \code{mod} in Disco.}

\p{Note, however, that the #{\div} and #{\mod} operators in Java, C/C++, and Rust do not work in the same way as specified above.  In particular, in those languages, the result of #{a \mod d} is negative when #{a} is negative, rather than always returning a result in the range #{0 \leq r < d}.  Languages such as Python, Disco, and Haskell implement #{\mod} correctly.}
